WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.417
TONY PUJALS: You've
probably heard

00:00:01.417 --> 00:00:03.240
about Anthos, Google
Cloud's application

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:06.400
platform for modern
enterprise environments.

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:08.790
If you want a chance to
try Anthos for yourself,

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:11.140
the good news is now you can.

00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:12.780
I'm going to show
you exactly how easy

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:16.020
it is to get started exploring
Anthos using the Anthos sample

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:17.745
deployment.

00:00:17.745 --> 00:00:19.620
When we're done, go
ahead and follow the link

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:22.860
to our online tutorial, launch
your own sample deployment,

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:25.180
and try out Anthos for yourself.

00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:27.840
We have a sample
application, an online store

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:31.350
that's an example of a modern
micro services application that

00:00:31.350 --> 00:00:34.410
will run in the multi-cluster
Kubernetes environment created

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:36.510
by our deployment.

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:38.070
Everything I show
you and more is

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:41.250
published as a tutorial
in our online docs.

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:43.480
This is where you want
to go to get started.

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:46.210
What I'm going to show you will
be running in Google Cloud.

00:00:46.210 --> 00:00:47.760
But keep in mind
that Anthos is here

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:49.927
to help you build and manage
your modern application

00:00:49.927 --> 00:00:52.600
workloads running where
your enterprise needs them,

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:55.410
whether that's on Google
Cloud, multi-cloud,

00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:57.510
or in hybrid environments.

00:00:57.510 --> 00:00:59.370
This sample deployment
has two clusters

00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:02.680
for hosting copies of
our application services.

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:04.290
There's also a config
repo that we'll

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:06.660
use for maintaining all
configuration under version

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:08.360
control.

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:11.460
We'll access Cloud Shell from
a browser to edit policies

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:13.790
in a clone of the config repo.

00:01:13.790 --> 00:01:16.560
We'll push our committed changes
to the upstream config repo,

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:18.900
and then they will get
automatically synchronized

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:22.440
across both clusters by
Anthos Config Management.

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:24.060
Let's dive right in.

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:27.030
We need a project for the
services and resources

00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:30.090
that the Anthos sample
deployment will use or create.

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:32.940
For this tour, we'll
start with the new one.

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:36.700
And then let's navigate
to the Anthos dashboard.

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:39.630
We'll need to enable
the Anthos API.

00:01:39.630 --> 00:01:41.430
And then again back
at the dashboard,

00:01:41.430 --> 00:01:43.766
we can now launch
the deployment.

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:50.710
You can click through,
accept any defaults.

00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:52.280
After around 10
minutes, everything

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:56.090
will be deployed,
configured, and running.

00:01:56.090 --> 00:01:57.810
After the deployment
is finished,

00:01:57.810 --> 00:01:59.390
let's go back to
the Anthos dashboard

00:01:59.390 --> 00:02:02.210
to explore the infrastructure
resources that were created.

00:02:02.210 --> 00:02:04.230
From here, we can
see overall health.

00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:06.320
We can go ahead and
inspect our clusters

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:08.660
and drill down into
individual clusters.

00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:10.770
And then head over
to Kubernetes Engine,

00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:12.980
where we can see more details.

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:16.610
Drilling down into
physical nodes.

00:02:16.610 --> 00:02:21.830
Or we can see the pods
that are scheduled on them.

00:02:21.830 --> 00:02:24.320
Or take a look at
the actual workloads,

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:26.540
where we can see everything
running across all nodes

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:28.410
and namespaces.

00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:31.839
Or a specific namespace, like
the one for the application.

00:02:38.410 --> 00:02:40.680
Now let's take a look
at the web front end.

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:43.950
Services and ingress show us
a list of all the services

00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:46.030
with network endpoints.

00:02:46.030 --> 00:02:48.870
Let's filter on istio
ingress gateway,

00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:50.640
which is the name of
the services hosting

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:54.050
the endpoints for our
application's front end.

00:02:54.050 --> 00:02:58.430
Click on one of these for
either of our clusters.

00:02:58.430 --> 00:03:00.857
Ingress manages inbound
traffic to our application.

00:03:00.857 --> 00:03:03.440
And we can see what that looks
like by clicking on the exposed

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:03.940
endpoint.

00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:09.130
Looks like the
application is working.

00:03:09.130 --> 00:03:11.210
Let's switch view now.

00:03:11.210 --> 00:03:13.910
This web application is made
up of a number of communicating

00:03:13.910 --> 00:03:14.930
micro services.

00:03:14.930 --> 00:03:17.180
So let's see how things look
from the internal traffic

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:19.850
perspective of
Anthos Service Mesh.

00:03:19.850 --> 00:03:22.520
Back at the Anthos dashboard,
let's inspect services

00:03:22.520 --> 00:03:24.380
managed by the service mesh.

00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:26.660
We've got two views
available, the default table

00:03:26.660 --> 00:03:28.640
view and a topology view.

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:30.650
These views provide a
great deal of information

00:03:30.650 --> 00:03:32.570
without having to write
any custom monitoring

00:03:32.570 --> 00:03:34.250
code whatsoever.

00:03:34.250 --> 00:03:36.980
The table view presents us
with essential service metrics,

00:03:36.980 --> 00:03:39.890
like error rate and
percentile latency,

00:03:39.890 --> 00:03:41.510
while the topology
view presents us

00:03:41.510 --> 00:03:46.770
with a graph visualization of
our workloads and services.

00:03:46.770 --> 00:03:48.770
From either view, we
have a way to drill down

00:03:48.770 --> 00:03:50.870
into a detail page
for any service,

00:03:50.870 --> 00:03:53.960
as we can see here, using
the shipping service.

00:03:53.960 --> 00:03:56.810
In this service detail view,
we can see telemetry details

00:03:56.810 --> 00:03:59.940
that provide an overview of the
shipping services performance.

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:02.660
If we use the left nav and
click connected services,

00:04:02.660 --> 00:04:05.090
we can inbound and outbound
connections for our shipping

00:04:05.090 --> 00:04:05.893
service.

00:04:05.893 --> 00:04:08.060
We can even note that traffic
over these connections

00:04:08.060 --> 00:04:10.880
is unencrypted by looking
at the lock icons.

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:14.060
From these pages, we're able to
inspect service mesh details.

00:04:14.060 --> 00:04:16.190
But what happens when
there's an issue?

00:04:16.190 --> 00:04:19.700
Let's check out the monitoring
built into Anthos Service Mesh.

00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:21.470
At the table view,
we can see a summary

00:04:21.470 --> 00:04:24.050
at the top of the services we're
monitoring with the service

00:04:24.050 --> 00:04:26.307
level objective, or SLO.

00:04:26.307 --> 00:04:27.890
Right now it looks
like none are being

00:04:27.890 --> 00:04:29.045
monitored for performance.

00:04:29.045 --> 00:04:30.820
So let's fix that.

00:04:30.820 --> 00:04:32.570
We'll click into the
shopping cart service

00:04:32.570 --> 00:04:36.160
to create an SLO using latency
as our indicator performance

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:38.180
and choosing a very low
threshold, like five

00:04:38.180 --> 00:04:40.970
milliseconds with a
goal of 90% compliance

00:04:40.970 --> 00:04:42.555
over a one-day rolling period.

00:04:42.555 --> 00:04:44.180
This will let us see
an example of what

00:04:44.180 --> 00:04:46.400
it looks like when we have
service performance that

00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:48.830
exceeds our daily error budget.

00:04:48.830 --> 00:04:51.140
After we submit this
SLO, we can easily

00:04:51.140 --> 00:04:52.730
see it in the health panel.

00:04:52.730 --> 00:04:55.550
Let's go back to
our services table.

00:04:55.550 --> 00:04:57.860
We can see that we've
now upgraded to have

00:04:57.860 --> 00:05:00.410
only 10 services without SLOs.

00:05:00.410 --> 00:05:02.387
For the shipping cart
service with an SLO,

00:05:02.387 --> 00:05:04.220
we can see that our
intentionally aggressive

00:05:04.220 --> 00:05:07.640
objective has put us
past our error budget.

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:10.910
Now let's look at how to better
secure our service traffic.

00:05:10.910 --> 00:05:13.220
As an e-commerce
application, data security

00:05:13.220 --> 00:05:15.740
is critical to our
enterprise and customers.

00:05:15.740 --> 00:05:17.630
We need to ensure
our services are only

00:05:17.630 --> 00:05:19.350
using encrypted traffic.

00:05:19.350 --> 00:05:22.430
But recall that we noticed
earlier our shipping service

00:05:22.430 --> 00:05:25.430
had an open lock, revealing
that unencrypted traffic can

00:05:25.430 --> 00:05:26.750
pass through.

00:05:26.750 --> 00:05:29.270
Anthos lets us easily fix
this with an encryption

00:05:29.270 --> 00:05:31.530
policy applied
through configuration

00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:34.470
so we don't even have to
touch the service code.

00:05:34.470 --> 00:05:36.740
Let's activate Cloud Shell
so we can make some changes

00:05:36.740 --> 00:05:39.290
to configuration.

00:05:39.290 --> 00:05:42.102
To be able to access our project
and make changes from the Cloud

00:05:42.102 --> 00:05:44.060
Shell environment, we'll
need to ensure that we

00:05:44.060 --> 00:05:45.890
have a few tools installed.

00:05:45.890 --> 00:05:48.500
We'll be using kubectl, a
clone of our configuration

00:05:48.500 --> 00:05:51.590
repository, to synchronize
our changes and the kubeconfig

00:05:51.590 --> 00:05:53.330
environment for our project.

00:05:53.330 --> 00:05:54.872
We'll source an
initialization script

00:05:54.872 --> 00:05:57.247
into our shell environment
that takes care of all of this

00:05:57.247 --> 00:05:58.490
for us so we're ready to go.

00:06:01.700 --> 00:06:04.220
Once this is all done, let's
head to the config repo

00:06:04.220 --> 00:06:07.620
clone directory.

00:06:07.620 --> 00:06:10.710
Then let's use integrated
editor to quickly edit

00:06:10.710 --> 00:06:12.390
our mesh policy.

00:06:12.390 --> 00:06:15.780
So we can tighten
up our restrictions

00:06:15.780 --> 00:06:21.910
by getting rid of
permissive mode

00:06:21.910 --> 00:06:25.300
and by requiring mutual
TLS for all matching hosts.

00:06:29.560 --> 00:06:32.080
After we make these
configuration changes,

00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:37.330
we'll nomos vet to ensure
there aren't any other issues.

00:06:37.330 --> 00:06:42.190
Once we push our changes to the
upstream repository with git,

00:06:42.190 --> 00:06:44.728
Anthos config management
will quickly detect and apply

00:06:44.728 --> 00:06:45.895
the changes to our clusters.

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:51.310
We'll run a little
function helper

00:06:51.310 --> 00:06:55.630
to go ahead and watch
for those changes here.

00:06:55.630 --> 00:06:57.700
And that's how easy it
is to apply a policy

00:06:57.700 --> 00:06:59.860
and have Anthos Config
Management synchronize it

00:06:59.860 --> 00:07:02.720
across all the clusters
in your environment.

00:07:02.720 --> 00:07:05.470
Finally, we can return to
the connected services view.

00:07:05.470 --> 00:07:08.620
And after enough time
passes for the desired span,

00:07:08.620 --> 00:07:10.210
we can visually
confirm that we only

00:07:10.210 --> 00:07:15.370
had mTLS traffic with the green
locks for connected services.

00:07:15.370 --> 00:07:17.890
There are plenty of examples
of security guard rails

00:07:17.890 --> 00:07:19.750
you can set to protect
your application

00:07:19.750 --> 00:07:21.430
environment with Anthos.

00:07:21.430 --> 00:07:25.060
Start with a tutorial and check
out the docs to learn more.

00:07:25.060 --> 00:07:27.070
We've just taken a
short tour of Anthos

00:07:27.070 --> 00:07:28.750
using a sample deployment.

00:07:28.750 --> 00:07:31.220
This is just a taste of
things for you to try.

00:07:31.220 --> 00:07:33.970
So check out our tutorial,
launch a sample deployment,

00:07:33.970 --> 00:07:36.990
and explor Anthos for yourself.

